      "status": "ok",
      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The evidence addresses both shortcomings named in the abstract. For overconfidence, the evidence is partial: Related Work discusses label smoothing (which increases prediction entropy and prevents saturating softmax) and global normalization as alternative approaches, and the Introduction frames the sharp-prediction problem, so label smoothing is presented as a solution. For the partial-transcript problem, the 'Solutions to Partial Transcripts Problem' section explicitly describes three strategies: (1) forbidding EOS emission unless within a range of the most probable token, (2) extending beam search to promote long transcripts, and (3) a coverage term counting frames with cumulative attention above a threshold to prevent looping. The evidence fully supports the incomplete-transcript solutions and sufficiently supports label smoothing as the overconfidence remedy, though the specific dedicated section detailing the overconfidence solution is not present in full."
      },
      "elapsed_ms": 6454.197200015187
    },
